on. Taken together, feature_027 appears highly outcome_014-specific within this dataset, with at most a borderline cross-outcome signal for outcome_013.\n\nFor feature_020, the cross-outcome screens mostly fail to replicate weaker dependency across outcomes. H122 for outcome_013 is near null and its upper bound is below the outcome_013 threshold, so the weaker-dependency claim is rejected. H124 for outcome_015 is also near null with upper bound below the threshold, so it is rejected. H123 for outcome_014 is slightly negative and imprecise; the interval still permits some positive values, so I close it unresolved rather than claim meaningful evidence. H125 for outcome_012 is actually negative, signed -0.0205, with interval -0.0418 to +0.0007, so the original weaker-dependency direction is rejected; the possible opposite is not clinically secure enough to make a new accepted claim. By contrast, H128 tests the accepted outcome_006 feature_020 association within feature_027=0 and gives signed effect +0.0537, interval +0.0045 to +0.1029. The point estimate is clinically large and the interval excludes zero, although its lower bound is below the outcome_006 threshold. I accept H128 as supportive dataset-specific evidence that feature_020 is not merely mirroring feature_027, and I keep it active while pursuing independent validation of the cleaner unconditional claim H117.\n\nThe most valuable next validation is H117, feature_020=1 versus 0 for outcome_006 in the weaker-dependency direction. It has already been accepted on discovery evidence, is clinically meaningful, and is now supported by a mutual-conditioning screen showing persistence among feature_027=0. Independent validation would help determine whether this is a stable dataset-specific association rather than a broad-screen artifact or shared coding with feature_027. No causal or external generalizability claims are being made.",
